.TH AT 1 "2026-03-20" "Substrate OS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
at, batch \- queue, examine, or delete jobs for later execution
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B at
[\fB-m\fR | \fB-M\fR] [\fB-f\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB-q\fR \fIqueue\fR] \fItime\fR
.br
.B at -b
.br
.B batch
[\fB-m\fR] [\fB-f\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB-q\fR \fIqueue\fR] [\fItime\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B at
and
.B batch
read commands from standard input or a specified file which are to be executed at a later time, using
.BR sh (1).
.PP
.B at
executes commands at a specified time.
.PP
.B batch
executes commands when system load levels permit; in this implementation, it is synonymous with
.B at -q b -m now
under POSIX strict mode.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-m
Send mail to the user when the job has completed even if there was no output. This is the default in POSIX strict \fBbatch\fR invocations.
.TP
.B \-M
Never send mail to the user. (GNU extension)
.TP
.B \-f \fIfile\fR
Read the job from \fIfile\fR rather than standard input.
The file is opened with the invoking user's permissions.
.TP
.B \-q \fIqueue\fR
Use the specified queue. A queue designation consists of a single letter; valid queue designations range from `a' to `z' and `A' to `Z'. The `a' queue is the default for \fBat\fR and the `b' queue is the default for \fBbatch\fR.
.TP
.B \-b
Alias for \fBbatch\fR. (GNU extension)
.SH EXTENSIONS AND COMPATIBILITY
The Substrate implementation strictly separates POSIX behavior from historical extensions:
.SS POSIX Strict Profile
When invoked as bare \fBbatch\fR with no options or operands, the system strictly enforces POSIX.1-2024 compliance. It implicitly maps to queue `b`, implicitly implies \fB\-m\fR (always mail), and executes the script using the Bourne Shell (\fB/bin/sh\fR).
.SS BSD Extensions Profile
When invoked with options (e.g., \fBbatch \-m\fR or \fBbatch tomorrow\fR), the parser enters the BSD Extended Profile. In BSD compatibility mode, \fBbatch\fR may default to queue `V` or `E` depending on system tune, and will \fBnot\fR mail by default unless \fB\-m\fR is explicitly passed. BSD timespec extensions such as \fBteatime\fR, \fBnoon\fR, \fBmidnight\fR, and \fBnow\fR are accepted.
.SS GNU Extensions Profile
The \fBat\fR command supports GNU extensions natively, specifically \fB\-b\fR to simulate \fBbatch\fR, and \fB\-M\fR to suppress all mail notification.
.SH HISTORICAL QUIRKS
Unsupported historical quirks are explicitly rejected rather than silently inherited. If an ambiguous mixed-profile behavior is attempted (e.g., mixing GNU-specific tunables with BSD-specific queue default assumptions in a single invocation without explicit definition), the system will fail deterministically with a diagnostic error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/spool/at
The job spool, owned by root and mode 0700.
\fBat\fR and \fBbatch\fR are installed set-user-ID root to write it; they
run with the invoking user's identity except while creating the job file,
which belongs to that user.
.TP
.IR /etc/at.allow ", " /etc/at.deny
Who other than root may submit jobs: if \fIat.allow\fR exists, only the
users it lists; otherwise, if \fIat.deny\fR exists, everyone it does not
list; if neither exists, only root.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR crontab (1),
.BR sh (1)
